{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "On August 13, 2015, the Russian Government announced the expansion of the food embargo to include Albania, Montenegro, Iceland, Liechtenstein, and Ukraine.",
      "source_date": "2015-08-13"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "On June 2015, the Russian Government extended the original 2014 food embargo on imports from the US, EU, Canada, Australia, and Norway to remain effective until August 5, 2016.",
      "source_date": "2015-06-01"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "As of August 2015, there were no Russian government statements indicating a potential lifting of food sanctions; instead, official statements confirmed the expansion and extension of the embargo.",
      "source_date": "2015-08-13"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "On July 29, 2015, President Vladimir Putin signed Presidential Decree No. 391, which mandated the destruction of banned products found in the Russian market, reinforcing the strict enforcement of the embargo.",
      "source_date": "2015-07-29"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "The embargo was initially imposed in August 2014 as a one-year ban and was subsequently extended in 2015 with the explicit possibility of another one-year extension beyond August 2016.",
      "source_date": "2015-06-01"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "Based on the evidence, the Russian government explicitly extended the food embargo on the US, EU, Canada, Australia, and Norway until August 5, 2016, in June 2015 [2]. Furthermore, in August 2015, the embargo was expanded to include Iceland, Albania, Montenegro, and Liechtenstein [1]. Official statements from the Russian government in 2015 confirmed the extension and expansion of the embargo rather than any intention to lift it [3]. The signing of Presidential Decree No. 391 in July 2015 reinforced the strict enforcement of the ban by mandating the destruction of banned products [4]. Given the explicit extension to August 5, 2016, and the lack of any statements suggesting a lift prior to that date, it is highly unlikely that Russia would officially lift any part of the embargo before August 6, 2016.",
  "probability_yes": 0.05,
  "prediction": "no"
}

{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "On March 19, 2020, Goldenvoice officially announced that Coachella 2020 was postponed from April to October 9–11 and October 16–18, 2020, due to COVID-19 concerns.",
      "source_date": "2020-03-19"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "As of March 19, 2020, the festival was not cancelled, only postponed, with plans to hold it in October pending further developments related to the pandemic.",
      "source_date": "2020-03-19"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "On March 11, 2020, California Governor Gavin Newsom announced that mass gatherings of 250 people or more should be postponed or canceled statewide until at least the end of March.",
      "source_date": "2020-03-11"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "As of March 19, 2020, Goldenvoice stated that all original ticket passes would be honored for the new October dates, and a full refund option was offered for those who could not attend.",
      "source_date": "2020-03-19"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "Based on the evidence, Coachella 2020 was officially postponed to October 2020 on March 19, 2020 [1][2]. At that time, the organizers explicitly stated the event was not cancelled but rescheduled, with plans to proceed in October pending further pandemic developments [2]. While California had issued restrictions on mass gatherings in March [3], the specific announcement for Coachella was a postponement to October, not a cancellation. There is no publicly available information before the cutoff date (March 20, 2020) indicating that the October dates would be postponed again or cancelled before October 9, 2020. The status as of the cutoff was a confirmed postponement to October, with no indication of a subsequent cancellation or postponement prior to the event dates.",
  "probability_yes": 0.0,
  "prediction": "no"
}

{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "The #NoFees4Charity campaign was launched on December 1, 2020, to pressure Visa to eliminate credit card fees on donations to registered charities.",
      "source_date": "2020-12-01"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "Prior to December 1, 2020, there was no public record of a formal response from Visa to the #NoFees4Charity campaign.",
      "source_date": "2020-12-01"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "Visa maintained a policy in 2020 that charitable donations processed via credit cards are subject to the same standard merchant processing fees as other transactions, with no fee-free or reduced-rate program for charities.",
      "source_date": "2020-11-15"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "The campaign organizers explicitly stated they were not anticipating a negotiation with Visa, viewing the effort primarily as a social media pressure tactic rather than a direct negotiation process.",
      "source_date": "2020-12-01"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "Visa's standard fee structure for merchants, including charities, typically ranges from 1.5% to 3.5% depending on the card type and merchant agreement, with no special donation fee waiver program available before December 2020.",
      "source_date": "2020-11-15"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "The #NoFees4Charity campaign was launched on December 1, 2020 [1], and the resolution criteria require a public announcement by Visa by December 15, 2020. Prior to the campaign's launch, there was no record of Visa responding to these specific fee concerns [2]. Visa's established position in 2020 was that charitable donations are subject to standard merchant processing fees with no special waiver programs [3][5]. Furthermore, the campaign organizers themselves did not anticipate a negotiation, viewing the effort as a social media pressure tactic rather than a process likely to yield immediate results [4]. Given that the campaign had just begun on the first day of the resolution window and Visa had no prior history of such fee reductions or special programs for charities, it is highly improbable that Visa would issue a public announcement reducing fees by 50% or more within the 14-day window solely in response to a newly launched social media campaign.",
  "probability_yes": 0.05,
  "prediction": "no"
}

{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "As of early September 2017, the VIX index was at historically low levels, sitting near its 99th percentile low over the preceding 30 years.",
