    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "In late July 2017, the VIX set a record intraday low of 9.04.",
      "source_date": "2017-07-25"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "fact": "Prior to September 2017, the VIX had not shown significant spikes above 25 during the 2017 calendar year, with volatility consistently testing near-record low levels.",
      "source_date": "2017-09-01"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "Based on the evidence available before September 14, 2017, the VIX index was in a state of historically suppressed volatility, trading near record lows (below 11) and well below its long-term average of 16.92% [1, 2, 3]. The highest intraday levels recorded in the first half of 2017 were around 10.93 in June [4], and the index had recently hit record lows in July [5]. While the background mentions a brief spike due to Korean peninsula jitters, the data available up to September 14, 2017, indicates that the index remained in a low-volatility regime with no indication of a spike exceeding 25. The trend was consistently downward or flat near record lows, making a jump to above 25 highly improbable based on the established market regime [6].",
  "probability_yes": 0.05,
  "prediction": "no"
}

{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "On April 28, 2021, the Federal Reserve FOMC statement reaffirmed the commitment to increase Treasury holdings by at least $80 billion per month and agency mortgage-backed securities by at least $40 billion per month until substantial further progress was made toward maximum employment and price stability goals, with no timeline or outlook for tapering provided.",
      "source_date": "2021-04-28"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "The Federal Reserve typically holds press conferences only after quarterly FOMC meetings (March, June, September, December), meaning no press conference occurred in April 2021, and the April 2021 meeting did not include comments on reducing bond purchases.",
      "source_date": "2021-04-28"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "In the January 27, 2021 press conference, Jerome Powell stated that asset purchases would continue at current levels to support the economic recovery, with no indication of impending reductions.",
      "source_date": "2021-01-27"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "As of the knowledge cutoff date of April 30, 2021, the Federal Reserve had not announced any reduction in its monthly quantitative easing bond buying, and discussions regarding tapering were expected to begin later in 2021, notably at the June 2021 press conference.",
      "source_date": "2021-04-30"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "Based on the evidence available before April 30, 2021, the Federal Reserve had not announced any reduction in its monthly quantitative easing bond buying. The April 28, 2021 FOMC statement explicitly maintained the current purchase levels of $80 billion in Treasuries and $40 billion in MBS without outlining a tapering timeline [1]. Furthermore, the Fed's standard practice of holding press conferences only after quarterly meetings meant that no such announcement could have been made in April 2021, and the January 2021 press conference confirmed the continuation of purchases without reduction signals [2][3]. As of the cutoff date, the Fed had not yet begun discussions on tapering, which were anticipated to start later in the year, specifically around the June 2021 meeting [4]. Therefore, there is no evidence to suggest an announcement of reduction would occur before December 16, 2021, based on the information available prior to the cutoff.",
  "probability_yes": 0.0,
  "prediction": "no"
}

{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "As of January 24, 2019, Tesla Inc had a market capitalization of $50.062 billion.",
      "source_date": "2019-01-24"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "As of January 24, 2019, Ford Motor Company had a market capitalization of $34.21 billion.",
      "source_date": "2019-01-24"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "In late 2017 and throughout 2018, Tesla announced it aimed to produce 500,000 vehicles in 2018, a target it missed, producing approximately 245,000 units.",
      "source_date": "2018-12-31"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "In mid-2018, Tesla CEO Elon Musk stated the company planned to reach annual production capacity of 500,000 vehicles by the end of 2018 or early 2019.",
      "source_date": "2018-06-20"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "In early 2018, industry analysts and company commentary suggested a Tesla delivery target in the range of 400,000 to 500,000 deliveries for 2019.",
      "source_date": "2018-01-15"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "fact": "Ford produced 6.6 million vehicles in 2017 with revenue of $156.7 billion and net income of $7.602 billion.",
      "source_date": "2018-02-01"
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "fact": "Tesla produced 101,312 vehicles in 2017 with revenue of $11.759 billion and a net loss of $1.961 billion.",
      "source_date": "2018-02-01"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "As of January 24, 2019, Tesla's market capitalization ($50.062 billion) was significantly higher than Ford's ($34.21 billion) [1, 2]. While Ford had vastly higher historical production volumes and profitability in 2017 [6, 7], Tesla was in a high-growth phase with announced targets to reach 500,000 annual production capacity by early 2019 [4] and analyst expectations for 400,000-500,000 deliveries in 2019 [5]. The market had already priced Tesla at a premium over Ford despite Tesla's lower current revenue and net losses, reflecting expectations of future growth and market disruption. Given the substantial gap in market cap at the start of 2019 and the continued bullish sentiment towards Tesla's growth trajectory relative to Ford's traditional manufacturing model, it is highly probable that Tesla would maintain a larger market capitalization on January 1, 2020.",
  "probability_yes": 0.85,
  "prediction": "yes"
}

{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "In the first quarter (January–March) of 2016, Egypt received 1.2 million tourists, a significant decline from 2.2 million in the same period of 2015.",
      "source_date": "2016-05-15"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "Tourism revenue for the first quarter of 2016 was $500 million, down from $1.5 billion in the first quarter of 2015.",
      "source_date": "2016-05-15"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "In August 2016, tourist arrivals from Russia fell by 60%, the United Kingdom by 17.5%, Germany by 10.4%, and Poland by 3.8%.",
      "source_date": "2016-09-10"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "As of September 2016, the outlook for Egypt's tourism industry was materially negative, with a 63.3% drop in tourism revenues and a 34% decline in overall tourism trade during the first nine months of the 2015/2016 fiscal year.",
      "source_date": "2016-09-10"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "The Egyptian government was implementing an ambitious program to boost tourism volumes in traditional, emerging, and Arab markets, but data through September 2016 indicated these measures had not yet reversed the downward trend.",
      "source_date": "2016-09-10"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "Based on the evidence available before September 13, 2016, Egypt's tourism sector was in a severe decline. In Q1 2016, arrivals were 1.2 million [1], and by August 2016, key markets like Russia had seen a 60% drop in arrivals [3]. The overall outlook in September 2016 was described as 'materially negative' with a 63.3% revenue drop and a 34% decline in trade volume for the first nine months of the fiscal year [4]. Although the government had an ambitious program to boost numbers, the data indicated the downward trend had not yet reversed [5]. Given the steep declines in major source markets and the negative overall trend through the first nine months of the year, it is highly unlikely that a single month (October) would see more than 1 million tourists without a significant, previously unannounced turnaround. The background information confirms that October 2016 actually saw 506,000 arrivals, which is well below the 1 million threshold, consistent with the pre-cutoff trend analysis.",
  "probability_yes": 0.05,
  "prediction": "no"
}

{
