{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "In August 2017, FiveThirtyEight's aggregate tracker for Donald Trump's job approval rating showed approximately 38.1% for all polls, with 39.7% among likely/registered voters and 36.7% among all adults.",
      "source_date": "2017-08-10"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "Individual polls from early August 2017 showed Trump's approval at 37% (Ipsos/Reuters, August 2) and 36% (NBC News/Wall Street Journal, August 5–9).",
      "source_date": "2017-08-09"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "Donald Trump's first approval rating recorded by Gallup was 45% on January 22, 2017, shortly after his inauguration.",
      "source_date": "2017-01-24"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "Historical data indicates that approval ratings often decline from the first to the second year due to the end of the 'honeymoon period' and emerging policy controversies.",
      "source_date": "2017-01-20"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "Barack Obama's approval rating dropped from 69% in late January 2009 to approximately 48% by the end of 2009.",
      "source_date": "2017-01-20"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "fact": "George W. Bush started his presidency with approximately 64% approval in 2001 and ended with 34% in January 2009.",
      "source_date": "2017-01-20"
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "fact": "Bill Clinton had a sub-40% approval rating six months into his presidency but eventually rebounded to 60% by the end of his presidency.",
      "source_date": "2017-01-20"
    },
    {
      "id": 8,
      "fact": "Gerald Ford's approval rating tanked after his pardoning of Nixon but managed to barely crawl above 50% approval later in his term.",
      "source_date": "2017-01-20"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "As of August 2017, Trump's approval rating on FiveThirtyEight's aggregate tracker was approximately 38.1% [1], having dropped from an initial 45% in January 2017 [3]. Historical trends for modern presidents show a general pattern of declining approval from the first to the second year, with Obama dropping from 69% to 48% and Bush from 64% to 34% [4, 5, 6]. While there are exceptions where presidents rebounded, such as Bill Clinton and Gerald Ford, these cases involved specific political recoveries or unique circumstances [7, 8]. Given the data available before August 2017, Trump's rating was trending downward and was significantly below the 50% threshold. There was no evidence in the pre-cutoff data suggesting a rapid or sustained rebound to 50% or higher by November 2018, especially considering the context of ongoing scandals and the typical historical decline in the second year of a presidency.",
  "probability_yes": 0.15,
  "prediction": "no"
}

